---
/**
 * Hero — SPEC section 9, ARCHITECTURE section 6.
 *
 * TWO STATES, AND THE NO-FOOTAGE STATE IS STILL THE DEFAULT PATH. With no
 * poster in `src/assets/` this renders exactly the type-led hero it rendered
 * before Phase 10 built the media layers: no <video>, no <picture>, no scrim,
 * no reserved band, no aspect-ratio box. It is a type-led hero that GAINS a
 * video, not a video hero with a hole in it, and that distinction is why the
 * media layers are gated on the assets existing rather than shipped pointing
 * at files that may not be there.
 *
 * THE TWO ASSETS LIVE IN DIFFERENT PLACES AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, NOT AN
 * INCONSISTENCY. Do not "tidy" them into one directory:
 *
 *   src/assets/hero-poster.jpg   Astro PROCESSES it. That is what produces the
 *                                AVIF/WebP srcset, the intrinsic width and
 *                                height, and the build error if it goes
 *                                missing. Nothing in public/ can be processed.
 *   public/video/hero.mp4        Astro does NOT process video, so there is
 *                                nothing to gain from src/ — and check 7 in
 *                                scripts/check-html.mjs enforces the 1.5 MB cap
 *                                against this exact path.
 *
 * Swapping in real footage is: drop both files at those two paths. No code
 * change, the same contract BeforeAfter has.
 *
 * unconfirmed(footage): the hero footage comes out of the FIRST CONTROLLED
 * FLIGHT on family property, flown as soon as the ordered kit arrives. That is
 * a SEQUENCE and it must not be rewritten as a date. This marker lives in the
 * repo, never on the page — a visitor sees a finished hero either way, and a
 * future session does not mistake that for unfinished work.
 *
 * What this must never acquire: stock footage, a stock photograph, an
 * illustration of a drone we do not own, a grey box, or the words "video
 * coming soon". SPEC section 4 and CLAUDE.md apply to the hero identically.
 *
 * The price and the credentials are read here, never passed in — the pairing of
 * the headline with the published price is the whole point of the section.
 */
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs'
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets'
import { site } from '../config/site'
import { homeCopy } from '../i18n/home'
import { formatPrice, path, useTranslations } from '../i18n/utils'
import type { Locale } from '../i18n/ui'

interface Props {
  locale: Locale
  headline: string
  sub: string
}

const { locale, headline, sub } = Astro.props
const t = useTranslations(locale)
const c = homeCopy[locale].hero

/**
 * The written channel SPEC section 3 asks for. This was a conditional falling
 * back to `mailto:site.email` until Phase 6 built the form — and that fallback
 * is removed, not merely unreachable, because a dead branch anyone could revive
 * by touching `builtRoutes` is worse than no branch. That reasoning stands on
 * its own and does not depend on the address: `site.email` works as of 26 July
 * 2026, and the hero's ask is still the form.
 */
const quoteHref = path('contact', locale)

/**
 * DOES THE FOOTAGE EXIST? Two gates, because the two assets live in two places
 * and only one of them is something Astro can import.
 *
 * The poster is found with `import.meta.glob`, not a bare `import`. A static
 * import of a file that is not there is a build error, and the no-footage state
 * is not an error — it is the launch state. A glob that matches nothing returns
 * `{}`, which is the clean "no footage yet" answer. `eager` is what makes the
 * value a real `ImageMetadata` rather than a promise, which is what `<Picture>`
 * needs. The extension list is broad so the operator can drop a JPEG, a PNG or
 * an AVIF without editing this file.
 */
const posterModules = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  '../assets/hero-poster.{jpg,jpeg,png,webp,avif}',
  { eager: true },
)
const poster = Object.values(posterModules)[0]?.default

/**
 * The video is a plain `existsSync` at build time, because `public/` is copied
 * rather than processed and there is nothing to import. The path is relative to
 * the project root, which is the cwd Astro builds in.
 *
 * THE VIDEO IS GATED ON THE POSTER TOO, not only on itself. The poster is the
 * LCP element and the thing the visitor actually sees; a video with no poster
 * would paint an empty box before it has decoded a frame, and would show
 * nothing at all to anyone whose playback never starts — which is every phone
 * under reduced motion, every visitor without JavaScript, and every visitor
 * whose fetch fails. There is no state in which a video without a poster is
 * better than no media at all.
 */
const hasVideo = Boolean(poster) && existsSync('public/video/hero.mp4')
const hasMedia = Boolean(poster)

/**
 * ARCHITECTURE section 2's THIRD named client-JavaScript exception, and the
 * second actually taken. Do not read it as a violation of the zero-JS rule and
 * delete it — and do not read it as a precedent for a fourth.
 *
 * It defers WHEN THE VIDEO PLAYS, never WHAT THE PAGE LOADS, which is the only
 * thing that was permitted. With JavaScript disabled there is no observer,
 * nothing calls play(), `preload="none"` means the mp4 is never fetched, and
 * the visitor keeps the poster — a finished state by design, not a broken one.
 * That is the same test the quote form's exception passes: it degrades to a
 * designed state rather than to nothing.
 *
 * IT IS A STRING HERE, AND NOT INLINE IN THE TEMPLATE, FOR ONE MECHANICAL
 * REASON. It has to be emitted only alongside the media — a page with no
 * footage has nothing to observe and must ship no script at all — and an
 * element inside a `{cond && ...}` expression has its children parsed as JSX,
 * where the braces and `<` in real JavaScript do not survive. `set:html` on the
 * script below emits it verbatim. If the condition ever goes away, this can go
 * straight back into the template the way QuoteForm's does.
 */
const viewportPlaybackScript = `
// Guard one, in its script form. Guard one proper is the \`media\` attribute on
// <source>, which leaves nothing to fetch under reduced motion — but a browser
// that ignores \`media\` there would fetch and play anyway, and with a scripted
// trigger that hole costs one line to shut. Guard two (the CSS rule) is
// untouched and still independent of both.
if (!matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
  for (const video of document.querySelectorAll('video.hero__video')) {
    // PAUSE ON EXIT, RESUME ON RE-ENTRY, so the observer is deliberately NOT
    // disconnected after the first entry. Browsers throttle timers off-screen
    // but keep decoding video, which on a phone means continuous decode for the
    // rest of the session for something nobody is looking at.
    new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      for (const entry of entries) {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          // play() rejects when guard one left no matching <source>, and on any
          // codec or network failure. Swallowing it is the whole fallback: no
          // \`is-playing\` class is added, so the poster never fades.
          video.play().then(() => video.classList.add('is-playing')).catch(() => {})
        } else {
          video.pause()
        }
      }
    }, { threshold: 0.25 }).observe(video)
  }
}
`
---
<section class:list={['hero', hasMedia && 'hero--media']}>
  {
    hasMedia && (
      <div class="hero__media" aria-hidden="true">
        {hasVideo && (
          /* NO `autoplay`, and NO `poster` attribute.
             `autoplay` is absent because playback is started by the observer at
             the foot of this file, on viewport entry — ARCHITECTURE section 2's
             third client-JavaScript exception.
             The `poster` ATTRIBUTE is absent because it takes a single URL and
             cannot carry a srcset; the poster is the <Picture> below it, which
             can. Using both would fetch two stills and show the wrong one.
             `muted` is not decoration: without it a scripted play() is refused
             without a user gesture, and iOS takes the video fullscreen without
             `playsinline`. */
          <video class="hero__video" muted loop playsinline preload="none">
            {/* GUARD ONE — the fetch never happens. When no <source> matches,
                the browser has nothing to load and play() rejects, so the
                poster simply stays. There is deliberately NO width condition
                here: excluding phones is a product decision rather than a
                performance guard, and most of this site's traffic is mobile.
                The video is preload="none" and fetched on viewport entry, so it
                never competes with the poster for LCP. If PageSpeed mobile on
                the DEPLOYED site drops below 90, re-add `(min-width: 48em)`
                citing that number — see PLAN Phase 10. */}
            <source
              src="/video/hero.mp4"
              type="video/mp4"
              media="(prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)"
            />
          </video>
        )}

        {/* The LCP element. Eager, high priority, never lazy — it is the first
            thing in the viewport and deferring it would be deferring the paint
            the whole page is judged on. `alt=""` because the media is
            decoration: every claim it makes visually is also made in the text
            beneath it, which is what keeps this hero working for a screen
            reader and with the media blocked.

            WRAPPED IN A DIV WE OWN, deliberately. <Picture> puts `class` on the
            inner <img> rather than on the <picture> it emits, so styling or
            selecting the poster through it would depend on that internal
            detail. The wrapper is a real element in this template, so Astro's
            style scoping reaches it and the adjacent-sibling rule below is
            stable whatever <Picture> emits inside. */}
        <div class="hero__poster">
          <Picture
            src={poster!}
            formats={['avif', 'webp']}
            widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1920]}
            sizes="100vw"
            alt=""
            loading="eager"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>

        {/* The scrim. Uniform, one token, tuned in tokens.css and nowhere else. */}
        <div class="hero__scrim" />
      </div>
    )
  }

  <div class="hero__inner">
    <ul class="hero__pills">
      {c.pills.map((claim) => <li class="pill">{claim}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="hero__sub">{sub}</p>

    <div class="hero__actions">
      <a class="btn btn--accent" href={quoteHref}>{t('cta.quote')}</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={site.phoneHref}>{site.phone}</a>
    </div>

    <p class="hero__price">
      {c.priceLead} <b>{formatPrice(site.prices.roofFrom, locale)}{c.priceUnit}</b> · {c.vatNote}
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The glow moved OFF this shorthand and onto ::after below, so it can paint
     ABOVE the video and its scrim. In the no-footage state the two forms are
     pixel-identical — the same gradient over the same base colour — which was
     confirmed by screenshot comparison rather than assumed. */
  .hero {
    background: var(--bg);
    text-align: center;
    /* Clears the absolutely positioned header pill in its overlay variant. */
    padding-block: calc(var(--sp-12) + var(--sp-7)) var(--sp-11);
    /* The media layers are absolutely positioned against this. `isolation`
       keeps them in their own stacking context so they cannot rise past the
       header, which is fixed at z-index 60 in Header.astro. */
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Room for the footage to read as footage. Without it the 16:9 frame is
     cropped to a strip by a hero sized to its type. Only in the media state —
     the no-footage hero's height is its type's, exactly as before. `svh` so a
     phone's collapsing browser chrome cannot make it jump; the `vh` line above
     is the fallback for anything that does not know `svh`. */
  .hero--media {
    min-height: 78vh;
    min-height: min(78svh, 720px);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /* ---- the media layers ----
     Both are `position: absolute`, so they add no height and nothing here can
     shift when the page loads. */
  .hero__media {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  .hero__video,
  .hero__poster {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .hero__video {
    z-index: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* ABOVE the video, and faded out once playback actually starts — so there is
     never a frame in which an undecoded <video> paints an empty box. If
     playback never starts, for any reason at all, this simply stays: no
     JavaScript, reduced motion, a failed fetch and an unsupported codec all
     land on the same finished poster-only hero. */
  .hero__poster {
    z-index: 1;
    transition: opacity 0.4s ease;
  }

  .hero__video.is-playing + .hero__poster {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* `:global` because these are <Picture>'s own output, not elements in this
     template, so the scope attribute never reaches them. */
  .hero__poster :global(picture),
  .hero__poster :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .hero__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    background: var(--hero-scrim);
  }

  /* GUARD TWO — no motion is ever visible. Independent of guard one on
     <source>, and not a fallback for it: guard one saves the bandwidth but
     depends on `media` being honoured, and guard two is plain CSS that has
     worked for a decade. Neither is dropped because the other exists. Hiding
     the video also drops `.is-playing + .hero__poster`, so the poster stays
     opaque even if playback somehow started. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hero__video {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* The radial wash, now a layer of its own so it can sit above the scrim.
     Static, never animated, never an image.

     OVER --bg IT LIFTS; OVER FOOTAGE IT DARKENS. Same token, same geometry,
     both times concentrating attention on the type — --hero-glow is darker
     than most frames, so against a video the identical layer reads as a
     vignette rather than a glow. That is the decision, and it is why the glow
     and the video are not fighting: they are not competing for the same job.
     (No hex here on purpose: the value lives in tokens.css and a copy in a
     comment is a copy that can drift.) */
  .hero::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: radial-gradient(90% 70% at 50% 0%, var(--hero-glow) 0%, transparent 60%);
  }

  .hero__inner {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 4;
    max-width: 920px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
    width: 100%;
  }

  .hero__pills {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    margin-block-end: var(--sp-6);
  }

  .hero__sub {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: var(--sp-5) auto 0;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-size: var(--step-sub);
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--sp-3);
    margin-block-start: var(--sp-8);
  }

  .hero__price {
    margin-block-start: var(--sp-7);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--step-sm);
  }

  .hero__price b {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: var(--step-sub);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  /* ---- the footage-state remap ----
   *
   * DO NOT DELETE THESE AS REDUNDANT. They are what makes the hero accessible
   * over footage, and they are measured, not stylistic. The scrim alone cannot
   * do it: against the worst case a frame can present — pure white, the same
   * bound --glass-media was derived against — the tokens these replace reach
   * 3.01:1 (pills), 2.26:1 (lead) and 1.76:1 (price), and NO alpha in the range
   * PLAN permits rescues any of them. The derivation and the full table are in
   * tokens.css beside --hero-scrim.
   *
   * The cost is that the hero loses its colour hierarchy whenever the video is
   * present, and carries it through size and weight instead. That is the same
   * deviation recorded at the head of tokens.css for Direction D's three dim
   * greys, taken for the same reason.
   *
   * Requirement to preserve if these are ever re-tuned: every piece of hero
   * text clears 4.5:1 against a PURE-WHITE frame, with no assumption made about
   * what the footage actually contains. Footage brightness is a quality
   * question, not this requirement — see PLAN Phase 10. */

  /* Glass over media, not over a surface we control — so --glass-media and
     --ink-on-glass, which BeforeAfter's Enne/Pärast tags already use for
     exactly this problem. 11.37:1 against a white frame. --pill-fill is a
     white tint and LIGHTENS the backdrop under the text, which is why the
     unremapped pill is the worst-performing element in the hero. */
  .hero--media .pill {
    background: var(--glass-media);
    border-color: var(--glass-line);
    color: var(--ink-on-glass);
  }

  .hero--media .hero__sub,
  .hero--media .hero__price {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Non-text contrast, WCAG 1.4.11. Both buttons need a boundary against the
     scrimmed video: --ghost-line falls to 1.76:1 and the accent fill to 2.95:1,
     both under the 3:1 threshold. --ink is 4.87:1 on the same backdrop and it
     is one rule for both. `.btn` already carries a transparent 1px border, so
     this changes no geometry and nothing shifts. */
  .hero--media .btn {
    border-color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>

{/* The third named exception. Reasoning is in the frontmatter above, which Astro
    discards rather than shipping; only what is needed to read the code is kept
    in the script itself.

    `is:inline` so Astro neither bundles nor hoists it — no extra request, the
    same treatment QuoteForm's script gets. Emitted ONLY when there is a video
    to observe, so the no-footage hero — which is the launch state — still ships
    no client JavaScript whatsoever. */}
{hasVideo && <script is:inline set:html={viewportPlaybackScript} />}
